---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import MathCanvas2D from "../../components/math-graphics/MathCanvas2D.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <section class="simple-page">
    <header class="page-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Math graphics laboratory</p>
      <h1>About</h1>
      <section class="topic-section" id="broad-stroke">
      <p>
	There's no grand point to this page, I just wanted to share some thoughts about the philosophy behind this website. I created Eigenward mainly as a way of dealing with the
seemingly insurmountable task of making sense of the things I seek to understand on a deeper level, for sheer knowledge's sake. I also happen to be someone with a
knack for trying WAY too hard to find a pleasing and fulfilling aesthetic for my personal notes before I even bother to read them through (which is ironically at odds with my
inherent lack of practical skills, and is why I prefer to digitalize all my stuff). So this place is the result of my desire to unify what I learn and tie it all up with a
pretty bow.
      </p>

      <p>
	Besides, look, I love 3Blue1Brown and the like as much as the next guy (this project is, more or less, the fruit of worshiping that kind of content), but over time I've grown
somewhat
frustrated with how little I actually retain from the exponentially increasing pool of visually stimulating and intuitive explanations of abstract topics out there on the internet.
That kind of content can be inspiring and oftentimes enlightening; it's just that it's also a rather passive and fickle way of supposedly wrapping your head around difficult subjects, and
at the end of the day it usually only scratches the surface of the insane number of bizarre or challenging conceptual ideas one can stumble upon. Most of the accessible presentations
I've seen have this introductory quality to them, and by the end they leave you staring at the untamed behemoth of pure, unadulterated theory that you still have to fully comprehend.
So on one hand I want to occasionally entertain you with what I hope are cool and elegant ways of looking at things you may have encountered in school or university—things you might
try explaining to a friend who's confused about them, only to realize you didn't actually remember any of the explanation yourself because, well... it just looked neat at the time and scratched
your brain in a satisfying way. So you might end up sending them the link to the page instead, only for you to most likely never reread it yourself, and for them to most likely never
read it in the first place. And on the other hand, I also want to get into the weeds with everything I explore here and provide textbook definitions, motivations for them, proofs,
problems, solutions, open questions, all the funny jazz fusion.
      </p>

      <p>
	Notice how I've purposely avoided using the term "Mathematics" so far. That's because of the somewhat pejorative, or perhaps foreboding, aura surrounding the word. It's such a
broad
field condensed into such a... controversial name that I'd rather refrain from using it as the primary way to describe what I'm doing here, simply because I know from experience that
it tends to have a Rorschach-test effect on people. And yet, on closer inspection, this is indeed where you'll find most of my attention directed, as it's what usually intrigues me the most.
      </p>

      <p>
	So this is partly an educational platform, if you choose to look at it that way, and partly an eclectic hodgepodge of all things abstract. Just keep in mind, as you explore,
that
I am by no means an authoritative figure on the topics I discuss, so please don't hesitate to let me know if you come across something profoundly stupid or simply incorrect. Enjoy!
      </p>
      </section>
    </header>

    <MathCanvas2D
      id="about-function-graph-scene"
      label="An explicit function graph with exact axis intersections and a draggable point"
      aspectRatio="16 / 9"
    />

    <MathCanvas2D
      id="about-trig-scene"
      label="Trig elements"
      aspectRatio="16 / 9"
    />
    
    <MathCanvas2D
      id="about-primitives-scene"
      label="Animated circles and polygons"
      aspectRatio="16 / 9"
    />

    <MathCanvas2D
      id="about-curve-scene"
      label="Trigonometry elements"
      aspectRatio="16 / 9"
    />

    <MathCanvas2D
      id="about-vector-drag-scene"
      label="Draggable vectors"
      aspectRatio="16 / 9"
    />

    <MathCanvas2D
      id="about-determinant-interactive-scene"
      label="Interactive determinant"
      aspectRatio="16 / 9"
    />

    <MathCanvas2D
      id="about-determinant-transformation-scene"
      label="Matrix transformation"
      aspectRatio="16 / 9"
    />

    <p class="scene-note">
      Every object is persistent geometry inside the Three.js scene graph.
      Motion and resizing alter object transforms, while outline tracing changes
      shader parameters rather than rebuilding geometry each frame.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import "../../scripts/demos/aboutTrigScene";
  import "../../scripts/demos/aboutPrimitivesScene";
  import "../../scripts/demos/aboutCurveScene";
  import "../../scripts/demos/aboutVectorDragScene";
  import "../../scripts/demos/aboutDeterminantInteractiveScene";
  import "../../scripts/demos/aboutDeterminantTransformationScene";
  import "../../scripts/demos/aboutFunctionGraphScene";
</script>

<style>
  .simple-page {
    width: min(72rem, calc(100% - 2rem));
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 5rem) 0;
  }

  .page-heading {
    max-width: 48rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.55rem;
    color: #91efff;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 760;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.8rem);
    line-height: 0.95;
  }

  .page-heading > p:last-child,
  .scene-note {
    color: rgba(239, 234, 255, 0.68);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .scene-note {
    max-width: 58rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.93rem;
  }
</style>
